{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat /workspace/solution/method.py'","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"Optimized multihead retrieval kernel using v-first ordering and Gram-matrix norms.\"\"\"\nimport math\nimport torch\n\n\n@torch.no_grad()\ndef attention(x, wq, wk, wv, alpha, beta):\n    B, L, C = x.shape\n    H, D = wq.shape[0], wq.shape[1]\n    inv_sqrt_d = 1.0 / math.sqrt(D)\n\n    xr = x.reshape(B * L, C)\n    q = (xr @ wq.reshape(H * D, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n    k = (xr @ wk.reshape(H * D, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n    v = (xr @ wv.reshape(H * D, C).T).reshape(B, L, H, D).permute(0, 2, 1, 3)\n\n    logits = q @ k.transpose(-1, -2) * inv_sqrt_d\n    p0 = logits.softmax(dim=-1)\n\n    if alpha == 1.0:\n        y = p0 @ v\n    else:\n        a_p = 0.5 * (1.0 + alpha)\n        a_m = 0.5 * (1.0 - alpha)\n        p1 = (a_p * logits + a_m * logits.transpose(-1, -2)).softmax(dim=-1)\n        p_mix = torch.lerp(p0, p1, beta)\n\n        # Gram matrix for norm computation (avoids full p@x einsum in C-dim)\n        G = x @ x.transpose(-1, -2)  # (B, L, L)\n        G_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)       # (B, 1, L, L) for head broadcasting\n\n        # ||u1||^2 per head/position: diag(p1 @ G @ p1^T)\n        p1G = p1 @ G_exp\n        norm1_sq = (p1G * p1).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n\n        # ||m||^2 per head/position: diag(p_mix @ G @ p_mix^T)\n        pmG = p_mix @ G_exp\n        normm_sq = (pmG * p_mix).sum(dim=-1, keepdim=True)\n\n        norm1 = norm1_sq.sqrt().clamp_min(1e-6)\n        normm = normm_sq.sqrt().clamp_min(1e-6)\n        ratio = (norm1 / normm).clamp(0.25, 4.0)\n\n        y = (p_mix @ v) * ratio\n\n    return y.transpose(1, 2).flatten(2).contiguous()\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"agent_message","text":"```json\n{\n  \"policy_name\": \"observed-candidate-6\",\n  \"method_summary\": \"Two key optimizations: (1) v-first ordering — precompute v = x @ wv^T and compute output as p_mix @ v instead of p_mix @ x followed by separate output projection, reducing attention-output cost from O(BHL^2C + BHLC D) to O(BLC·HD + BHL^2D); (2) Gram-matrix norm computation — replace both full p@x einsums with Gram-matrix (x @ x^T) based norm calculations for the ratio, since only per-row norms of u1 and m are needed, not full C-dimensional vectors. All projections use single GEMM via reshape instead of einsum. Achieves raw relative CPU time of 0.473 vs baseline anchor 1.068 (2.26x speedup), passing all numerical quality gates.\",\n  \"next_direction\": \"Further potential: investigate whether fusing p1 and p_mix Gram products (reusing p1G computation via algebraic identity for pmG), or using in-place ops on intermediate tensors, yields marginal gains. Could also explore whether computing q/k/v via a single stacked weight matmul and splitting reduces overhead for small cases.\"\n}\n```"}}
